WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.362
Interval and ratio variables can look similar because both are numeric and support meaningful differences.

00:00:06.362 --> 00:00:09.397
The major distinction is the zero point.

00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:14.287
Celsius zero is a reference point, not the absence of temperature.

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:17.397
Zero visits means no visits occurred.

00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:21.522
That difference determines whether ratio statements are meaningful.

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:28.077
When classifying a numeric variable, ask what zero means in the real-world measurement process.

00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:33.867
The easiest way to classify a variable is to ask what statements are meaningful in the real world.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:39.067
Can values only be different, can they be ordered, are differences meaningful, and does zero mean absence?

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:45.022
Those questions are more reliable than looking at whether the software stores the column as an integer or

00:00:45.022 --> 00:00:47.920
floating-point number.
